            <ArticleTitle>[Incidence and characteristics of lesions of extracranial segments of the brachiocephalic arteries in patients with ischemic heart disease].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>51-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The results of investigation are reviewed in 563 patients with angiographically-confirmed coronary arterial lesions. Combined affection of coronary and brachiocephalic arteries was recorded in 106 (18.8%) patients with isolated lesions of brain-supplying arteries in 59 and multiple lesions in 47. The incidence of the affection of brachiocephalic arteries increases with the involvement of the coronary bed, being 6.6% where one coronary artery is affected, 18.8% for two, and 23.0% for three affected coronary arteries. Ultrasonic dopplerography is of particular value for these patients as the most informative noninvasive procedure for the investigation of brachiocephalic arteries.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
